01:33:35.640 --> 01:33:40.080
seconds. They bring you hot tea when you're&nbsp;
done. They let you take your time to slowly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:40.080 --> 01:33:44.320
kind of come to. And then you can literally&nbsp;
walk out there and carry on with your day.&nbsp;

01:33:44.960 --> 01:33:48.920
And the first session I was like, "Okay,&nbsp;
that's beautiful." Second session was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:48.920 --> 01:33:51.840
little difficult. Because they caused it — they&nbsp;
say it kind of like, imagine it's loosening up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:51.840 --> 01:33:57.520
the plaque in your brain and rewiring circuits&nbsp;
and it's not always going to be easy. But by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:58.240 --> 01:34:03.400
I remember the sixth session, I just walked&nbsp;
out there and Dr. Jenn came in and she goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:03.400 --> 01:34:08.880
"How are you?" And I said, "I could run a&nbsp;
marathon right now. I feel amazing. I feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:08.880 --> 01:34:16.400
like a weight has been lifted off my chest. And&nbsp;
I just — this is such important work that you do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:16.400 --> 01:34:23.120
such important work." Because it's not about&nbsp;
being — there's not an addiction to the substance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:23.120 --> 01:34:29.320
I don't need to go back. Some people go back for&nbsp;
boosters depending on what they have. She told&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:29.320 --> 01:34:33.160
me that some people that have depression, they'll&nbsp;
come back in every three months, every six months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:33.160 --> 01:34:40.200
She said some people, she never sees again.
But it kind of takes the anxiety and pulls&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:40.200 --> 01:34:45.960
it apart from your body so that you can see it&nbsp;
for what it is, which is silliness because life&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:45.960 --> 01:34:54.520
is play. And when you realize life is play and&nbsp;
we're all here just trying to figure out our shit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:54.520 --> 01:34:56.880
why are we taking it so seriously?
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:56.880 --> 01:35:00.320
lot that just does not matter.
Kevin Rose: It doesn't matter. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:00.320 --> 01:35:06.240
can just chill, man, it doesn't really&nbsp;
matter. We have clean drinking water.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:06.240 --> 01:35:11.000
What the fuck are we complaining about?
Tim Ferriss: So the weight on the chest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:11.000 --> 01:35:16.800
was that something that you can't put words&nbsp;
too that you just felt release or — and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:16.800 --> 01:35:19.720
don't need to get into details, I'm&nbsp;
just curious. Or was there a content&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:19.720 --> 01:35:23.840
to it where you're like, "Oh, interesting."
Kevin Rose: No, it wasn't content. Content&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:23.840 --> 01:35:28.000
was beautiful. And I don't know if you've ever&nbsp;
experienced this, Tim with, I've never done&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:28.000 --> 01:35:34.160
ayahuasca, although at some point I would love to&nbsp;
try it. But I opened my eyes and it was when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:34.160 --> 01:35:41.360
mask is on and I was seeing things that were as&nbsp;
high of fidelity as what we see today, like right&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:41.360 --> 01:35:47.640
now. Where you're like, "I am in a room right&nbsp;
now." And I felt very present, my dad's passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:47.640 --> 01:35:54.560
away, I felt very present with a father source&nbsp;
there at times. I felt very connected. At one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:54.560 --> 01:36:00.800
point I saw the entire world and I saw how small&nbsp;
I was. And I was just like, it immediately gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:00.800 --> 01:36:05.360
me this sense of just gratitude for that being&nbsp;
that little speck, but also at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:05.360 --> 01:36:09.800
not having to take and carry the burdens of&nbsp;
the world on me for being that little speck.&nbsp;

01:36:09.800 --> 01:36:14.480
And so, there's bits and pieces of that, but I&nbsp;
would say at the end of the day, when you come&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:14.480 --> 01:36:23.160
out of it's not like you had this epiphany. It's&nbsp;
more like, Dr. Jenn calls it the ketamine's time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:23.160 --> 01:36:28.840
she calls it time-on brain. How can we make&nbsp;
this sit and do the rewiring on your brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:28.840 --> 01:36:33.120
and give you time-on brain with the drug and the&nbsp;
compound and let it do its work? And so it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:33.120 --> 01:36:38.080
a lot of surrendering. It was a lot of saying,&nbsp;
"You do what you want to do. I don't care where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:38.080 --> 01:36:43.720
you take me emotionally, mentally, whatever.&nbsp;
It doesn't really matter." It's time-on brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:43.720 --> 01:36:49.160
with a compound. And after a certain number&nbsp;
of sessions, you just feel this natural lift&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:49.160 --> 01:36:56.200
and lightness. And it felt like, I'm not a&nbsp;
ballerina, big surprise, but it felt like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:56.200 --> 01:37:02.547
little bit of walking through life is a little&nbsp;
bit of a dance now than it is such a struggle.&nbsp;

01:37:02.547 --> 01:37:04.400
Tim Ferriss: It makes me super&nbsp;
happy, man, to hear that.&nbsp;

01:37:04.400 --> 01:37:07.640
Kevin Rose: It feels amazing.
Tim Ferriss: And I remember getting the texts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:07.640 --> 01:37:12.600
some of the texts from you, and I was excited&nbsp;
to have this conversation. Which we haven't had,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:12.600 --> 01:37:14.907
this is the first time we're talking about it.
Kevin Rose: Yeah, it is.&nbsp;

01:37:14.907 --> 01:37:20.880
Tim Ferriss: And I'll say a few things. The first&nbsp;
is that, I have seen a number of cases where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:20.880 --> 01:37:29.360
ketamine therapy has changed or saved lives. So,&nbsp;
a friend of mine, for instance, who suffered from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:29.360 --> 01:37:36.560
depression his entire adult life had a similar&nbsp;
experience to yours. And he went to a clinic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:36.560 --> 01:37:43.360
I believe it was in New York City, very well&nbsp;
run. And he goes back once every six months for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:43.360 --> 01:37:47.094
a single tune-up, let's say just say —
Kevin Rose: Call it a booster, yeah.&nbsp;

01:37:47.094 --> 01:37:50.760
Tim Ferriss: — session. And I actually know,&nbsp;
I'm also not going to mention their name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:50.760 --> 01:37:56.080
but someone we know as a mutual friend who&nbsp;
you probably don't know also does this once&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:56.080 --> 01:38:05.240
every three to six months. And separately, I know&nbsp;
someone who's in law enforcement who is heavily&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:05.240 --> 01:38:13.960
dis-incentivized from talking about mental health&nbsp;
with his superiors because you'll be put on leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:13.960 --> 01:38:20.520
generally, if you even hinted that and you're in&nbsp;
certain professions, airline pilot for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:20.520 --> 01:38:24.760
you're going to be put on leave and it's —
Kevin Rose: "On leave" is a nice way&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:24.760 --> 01:38:26.360
to say you're probably fired.
Tim Ferriss: — it's a nice way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:27.640 --> 01:38:32.440
It's a career risk to bring these things&nbsp;
up, which puts many more people at risk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:32.440 --> 01:38:37.400
right? There should be a bit of processes for&nbsp;
this. But nonetheless, he was suicidal at one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:37.400 --> 01:38:41.880
point. We're talking about somebody who&nbsp;
was carrying around a firearm all day. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:41.880 --> 01:38:50.800
ketamine was an intervention that was incredibly&nbsp;
effective for him for pattern interrupting. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:50.800 --> 01:38:56.760
use that very literally, because the pattern was a&nbsp;
thought loop. This is personal, this is permanent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:56.760 --> 01:39:00.160
this is never going to change. And when&nbsp;
you're able to at least interrupt that for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:00.160 --> 01:39:04.480
a short period of time, you provide people with&nbsp;
hope or at least a window within which they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:04.480 --> 01:39:12.720
consider other options. So for acute suicide,&nbsp;
suicidal ideation, also for chronic pain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:12.720 --> 01:39:16.600
very interestingly, ketamine is super interesting.
Kevin Rose: Oh, yes. It's interesting you say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:16.600 --> 01:39:21.320
because Dr. Jenn over at this clinic has told&nbsp;
