01:40:35.320 --> 01:40:40.000
compromise your short-term memory, in the short&nbsp;
term, after a session, so you might forget where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:40.000 --> 01:40:42.467
your wallet is or forget where your backpack is.
Kevin Rose: I didn't have any of that.&nbsp;

01:40:42.467 --> 01:40:46.840
Tim Ferriss: Okay. So that can happen, and&nbsp;
that happened to me. And it happened to be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:46.840 --> 01:40:52.120
case that while I was doing these sessions,&nbsp;
because I shoehorned it into my schedule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:52.120 --> 01:40:57.240
I also had a number of huge podcasts coming&nbsp;
up like LeBron James and so on, and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:57.240 --> 01:41:02.476
freaking me out that I kept forgetting&nbsp;
shit, and so my anxiety was going up —&nbsp;

01:41:02.476 --> 01:41:03.320
Kevin Rose: Oh, that's amazing.
Tim Ferriss: Over the course of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:03.320 --> 01:41:06.640
treatment, which was not typical of me.
Kevin Rose: So I did the same thing. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:06.640 --> 01:41:10.410
gave me a whole breakdown. It was one&nbsp;
to five on a bunch of different scales.&nbsp;

01:41:10.410 --> 01:41:11.040
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Kevin Rose: I think it's a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:11.040 --> 01:41:16.000
standardized thing that they give you. I&nbsp;
can't remember what the anxiety scales are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:16.000 --> 01:41:20.840
but mine went down to literally a one on all of&nbsp;
them, or a zero on all of them, which is amazing.&nbsp;

01:41:20.840 --> 01:41:25.440
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. It is amazing. And I want to&nbsp;
mention a few other things. I'm sure Huberman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:25.440 --> 01:41:31.920
episode is excellent. For people who want to have&nbsp;
a comprehensive overview of ketamine, I'm sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:31.920 --> 01:41:34.547
Huberman's is great, so listen to that first.
Kevin Rose: It's fantastic.&nbsp;

01:41:34.547 --> 01:41:39.320
Tim Ferriss: I also did an episode&nbsp;
with John Krystal, Dr. John Krystal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:39.320 --> 01:41:45.320
who is the chairman of psychiatry at Yale, who&nbsp;
did a lot of the seminal research with respect&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:45.320 --> 01:41:50.840
to ketamine as an antidepressant in humans. So&nbsp;
the protocols that get used, which I think are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:50.840 --> 01:41:56.040
generally 0.5 milligrams per kilogram of body&nbsp;
weight, et cetera, over X period of time with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:56.040 --> 01:42:03.720
Y number of infusions, those are the protocols&nbsp;
that he developed with his other investigators.&nbsp;

01:42:03.720 --> 01:42:09.680
And I want to mention a number of things just&nbsp;
to make sure that I'm doing my safety-first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:09.680 --> 01:42:15.160
Ferriss duty. So the first is that part of&nbsp;
the reason, and this is pure speculation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:15.160 --> 01:42:21.760
but I think that it was risky for Matthew Perry to&nbsp;
use ketamine, is that he had a history of abuse.&nbsp;

01:42:21.760 --> 01:42:29.920
Ketamine can be very addictive for people&nbsp;
who are unwilling or uneager to feel certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:29.920 --> 01:42:34.920
things. It's a dissociative anesthetic. So if you&nbsp;
have, for instance, a history of alcohol abuse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:34.920 --> 01:42:41.680
it is, I would say, increasingly likely&nbsp;
that you might abuse ketamine, which is why,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:41.680 --> 01:42:49.548
if someone were to consider ketamine therapy,&nbsp;
I feel very strongly that it should be IV or —&nbsp;

01:42:49.548 --> 01:42:50.360
Kevin Rose: Yes.
Tim Ferriss: Intramuscular&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:50.360 --> 01:42:53.947
injection and not at-home treatment —
Kevin Rose: Oh, a hundred percent.&nbsp;

01:42:53.947 --> 01:42:57.209
Tim Ferriss: Where you have access&nbsp;
to, say, lozenges or a nasal spray.&nbsp;

01:42:57.209 --> 01:42:59.589
Kevin Rose: A hundred percent, because there&nbsp;
are companies that do lozenges at home.&nbsp;

01:42:59.589 --> 01:42:59.644
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Kevin Rose: Yeah, I completely agree.&nbsp;

01:42:59.644 --> 01:43:00.440
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah.
Kevin Rose: But I will say one thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:00.440 --> 01:43:05.667
Tim, real quick and I'll let you continue.&nbsp;
I noticed my desire for alcohol go down.&nbsp;

01:43:05.667 --> 01:43:12.040
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. I think that that can happen.&nbsp;
And I'm just saying for folks who, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:12.040 --> 01:43:15.747
may be coming out of or are part of AA —
Kevin Rose: Oh, yes.&nbsp;

01:43:15.747 --> 01:43:18.918
Tim Ferriss: And they have&nbsp;
issues with depression —&nbsp;

01:43:18.918 --> 01:43:20.320
Kevin Rose: Yeah, yeah, yeah.
Tim Ferriss: My personal take,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:20.320 --> 01:43:27.840
after everything I've seen, is that prior abuse&nbsp;
of alcohol highly correlated to potential abuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:27.840 --> 01:43:35.840
of ketamine. And I would say, furthermore,&nbsp;
there are some urological risks if you use&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:35.840 --> 01:43:44.000
ketamine chronically, and alongside that, if&nbsp;
you use ketamine chronically, and this is true&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:44.000 --> 01:43:47.680
for a lot of drugs, actually, not all of them.
Kevin Rose: That's for snorting though, right?&nbsp;

01:43:47.680 --> 01:43:51.835
Tim Ferriss: Do a self-check. I mean, snorting —
Kevin Rose: Because it wouldn't get in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:51.835 --> 01:43:53.307
urological tract, otherwise because&nbsp;
it would metabolize in the liver.&nbsp;

01:43:53.307 --> 01:43:56.354
Tim Ferriss: Well, most people who&nbsp;
consume ketamine recreationally —&nbsp;

01:43:56.354 --> 01:43:59.000
Kevin Rose: Oh, it would be their mouth, or —
Tim Ferriss: It will be a lot of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:59.000 --> 01:44:00.507
it will be snorting of one way —
Kevin Rose: Yeah, yeah, yeah.&nbsp;

01:44:00.507 --> 01:44:06.080
Tim Ferriss: Of one sort or another. So they'll&nbsp;
either have it in — it will be in suspension in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:06.080 --> 01:44:10.960
a liquid, or it will be a powder they snort&nbsp;
in the same way they would snort cocaine. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:10.960 --> 01:44:14.984
lot of people carry little lockets around their&nbsp;
necks with ketamine. I've seen this a lot and —&nbsp;

01:44:14.984 --> 01:44:17.491
Kevin Rose: And by the way, that's&nbsp;
not what I'm talking about here today.&nbsp;

01:44:17.491 --> 01:44:19.120
Tim Ferriss: No. No, no, no. I'm not —
Kevin Rose: But those are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:19.120 --> 01:44:20.840
two very different things.
Tim Ferriss: No, no. They are, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:20.840 --> 01:44:27.320
are. So just to be very clear, ketamine&nbsp;
therapy with IV or IM has a track record&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:27.320 --> 01:44:32.221
of being incredibly promising for a —
Kevin Rose: Trained physician —&nbsp;

01:44:32.221 --> 01:44:34.227
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, for a number of other things.
Kevin Rose: Like proper — yeah.&nbsp;

01:44:34.227 --> 01:44:43.680
Tim Ferriss: But where some folks get lost&nbsp;
is they go from, instead of clinical setting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:43.680 --> 01:44:50.360
