02:09:55.560 --> 02:10:00.028
to be disagreements. And by the way, if there&nbsp;
are no disagreements, something's wrong.&nbsp;

02:10:00.028 --> 02:10:00.640
Kevin Rose: Right.
Tim Ferriss: Right?&nbsp;

02:10:00.640 --> 02:10:03.160
Kevin Rose: A hundred percent.
Tim Ferriss: And therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:03.160 --> 02:10:09.720
having a shorthand and a set of agreements,&nbsp;
these are the rules that we agree to play by&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:09.720 --> 02:10:13.440
and we're not going to be perfect. I would&nbsp;
say that would be very high on the list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:13.440 --> 02:10:20.240
And I would say for really close relationships,&nbsp;
potentially, maybe we'll edit this out, but who&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:20.240 --> 02:10:26.680
knows? MDMA-assisted psychotherapy, let's just&nbsp;
say anywhere from once a quarter to once a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:26.680 --> 02:10:35.080
no more than once a quarter for a whole host of&nbsp;
reasons. But I think that while MDMA-assisted&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:35.080 --> 02:10:42.360
psychotherapy for PTSD is incredibly impressive,&nbsp;
and I've been very involved with that from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:42.360 --> 02:10:46.080
I don't want to say day one because that's not&nbsp;
true, but certainly for the last 10 years or so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:46.080 --> 02:10:52.400
I've been very involved up through phase three and&nbsp;
now onward. The results with treatment-resistant&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:52.400 --> 02:10:58.040
PTSD or a complex PTSD, where you see people&nbsp;
who've had this diagnosis for 17 years, suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:58.040 --> 02:11:02.440
after two or three sessions end up asymptomatic,&nbsp;
right? They would not meet the criteria for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:02.440 --> 02:11:09.320
PTSD with durability out to six, nine, 12 months.
I mean, it's something that almost defies belief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:09.320 --> 02:11:14.320
right? It is causing a complete reexamination&nbsp;
of psychiatry as we know it. I still think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:14.320 --> 02:11:22.560
is second place to couples work when it comes to&nbsp;
MDMA. I think that that is an incredibly fruitful&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:22.560 --> 02:11:29.160
arena for seeing the full potential of MDMA&nbsp;
psychotherapy. That would be another one. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:29.160 --> 02:11:34.880
sometimes, oftentimes couples end up, and I know&nbsp;
multiple people now who have had this experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:34.880 --> 02:11:39.880
with professional guidance, which by the way,&nbsp;
folks, currently is illegal. So be forewarned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:39.880 --> 02:11:43.760
there are legal consequences or at least risks&nbsp;
entailed with this. Not to mention the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:43.760 --> 02:11:49.520
that at least I would say 60 to 70 percent of&nbsp;
MDMA that you might purchase is adulterated or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:49.520 --> 02:11:53.560
mixed with something else at this point, so&nbsp;
you need to be very careful. Dancesafe.org&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:53.560 --> 02:11:57.880
is a resource I would recommend for testing kits&nbsp;
and so on if you're going to go that route. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:57.880 --> 02:12:01.120
not recommending you do anything illegal,&nbsp;
however, people are going to do it anyway.&nbsp;

02:12:01.120 --> 02:12:04.280
Kevin Rose: You have international listeners.
Tim Ferriss: People are going to do it anyway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:04.280 --> 02:12:08.160
I just recognize it's like you can't just&nbsp;
say to every teenager, "Don't have sex."&nbsp;

02:12:08.160 --> 02:12:08.680
Kevin Rose: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Kids are going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:08.680 --> 02:12:12.640
have sex. So let's be realistic about it.
Kevin Rose: I did that shit one time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:12.640 --> 02:12:14.548
It was fun as shit.
Tim Ferriss: Had sex?&nbsp;

02:12:14.548 --> 02:12:22.880
Kevin Rose: No. When I was 23, I did,&nbsp;
well, they were called ecstasy back then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:22.880 --> 02:12:28.240
but holy shit, that is a good compound.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, and that can also go&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:28.240 --> 02:12:30.507
sideways, just for the record.
Kevin Rose: Oh, no doubt.&nbsp;

02:12:30.507 --> 02:12:35.160
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. So, there are films out there&nbsp;
now, How to Change Your Mind, the miniseries on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:35.160 --> 02:12:39.147
Netflix. Watch the MDMA episode. It is excellent.
Kevin Rose: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:12:39.147 --> 02:12:42.880
Tim Ferriss: And it's pretty heavy because it gets&nbsp;
into some PTSD, but you'll be able to see live&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:42.880 --> 02:12:46.547
session footage if you want another alternative.
Kevin Rose: Man, Pollan's a hero.&nbsp;

02:12:46.547 --> 02:12:52.320
Tim Ferriss: He's great. And there is&nbsp;
also a doc called Trip of Compassion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:52.320 --> 02:12:56.840
which is worth seeing, which has a lot of&nbsp;
session footage as well. But those are a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:56.840 --> 02:13:01.680
of the things that would come to mind.
Let's say on a friendship level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:01.680 --> 02:13:08.760
I might suggest to my earlier self, let's just&nbsp;
say 30-year-old self, right? Something that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:08.760 --> 02:13:14.280
have really embraced and put into action in the&nbsp;
last handful of years, which is going to sound a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:14.280 --> 02:13:21.800
little anti-social, but I don't view it that way.
Humans have a finite capacity for building and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:21.800 --> 02:13:28.360
sustaining really deep relationships. You just&nbsp;
can't do that with everyone. And when I've looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:28.360 --> 02:13:34.200
back, say — and I do this every year at a past&nbsp;
year review, and I look back in my calendar every&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:34.200 --> 02:13:42.160
week of my last year, and on a piece of paper with&nbsp;
positive and negative, two columns, I write down&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:42.160 --> 02:13:49.320
the peak negative and positive experiences.
If I look at the commonalities for the peak&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:49.320 --> 02:13:54.960
positive experiences, it's usually the same 10&nbsp;
people, or fewer, right? It's the same cast of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:54.960 --> 02:14:03.360
characters. These are my close friends who&nbsp;
are nourishing, supportive, good influence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:03.360 --> 02:14:09.720
And before — what I would say to my younger self&nbsp;
is, "Before you seek to develop a bunch of new&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:09.720 --> 02:14:15.760
relationships, ask yourself, are you spending&nbsp;
enough time, as much time as you would like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:15.760 --> 02:14:21.480
with the people on that shortlist who you&nbsp;
know are guaranteed to be nourishing for you?"&nbsp;

02:14:21.480 --> 02:14:26.280
And if the answer is no, maybe you should double&nbsp;
down on those relationships. Maybe you should&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:26.280 --> 02:14:32.360
reach out to those people to get something on the&nbsp;
calendar before shit crowds it out, before you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:32.360 --> 02:14:38.520
look for shiny objects in new relationships. Which&nbsp;
doesn't mean I don't develop new relationships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:38.520 --> 02:14:47.680
I occasionally do, but I'm at a point where I&nbsp;
think recognizing the ephemeral nature of life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:47.680 --> 02:14:52.760
the finite limits, the constraints that&nbsp;
we have, is actually very enabling.&nbsp;

02:14:52.760 --> 02:15:01.680
It helps you to make cleaner, faster decisions.&nbsp;
So for me, I would just say, before seeking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:01.680 --> 02:15:07.120
develop new deep relationships, ask yourself&nbsp;
the question: "For my closest five friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:07.120 --> 02:15:12.640
let's just say, in the last year, did I spend as&nbsp;
much time as I would like with those people?" If&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:12.640 --> 02:15:16.987
the answer is no, reach out to those five first.
Kevin Rose: Yeah, yeah.&nbsp;

02:15:16.987 --> 02:15:20.200
Tim Ferriss: What about you?
Kevin Rose: Oh, man. I would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:20.200 --> 02:15:26.360
honestly, I think that we're at the age now&nbsp;
where every day just brings a new unknown&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:26.360 --> 02:15:32.720
in terms of how long are we going to last. I've&nbsp;
had multiple friends with cancer now. I had one&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:32.720 --> 02:15:40.160
friend that I almost lost this year that was in&nbsp;
the emergency room for weeks that we thought had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:40.160 --> 02:15:47.800
stage four cancer and it ended up being a horrible&nbsp;
bacterial infection from some foreign country.&nbsp;

02:15:47.800 --> 02:15:53.000
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I remember this.
Kevin Rose: I just realized there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:53.000 --> 02:15:59.040
so many times when we hang out and we just give&nbsp;
a hug and say, "Oh, good to see you, blah, blah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:59.040 --> 02:16:07.960
blah." But we don't say, "I love you." We don't&nbsp;
say, "You are so essential to me in so many ways."&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:07.960 --> 02:16:12.600
And maybe this is the ketamine talking, but like&nbsp;
— no, but when I went through this whole thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:12.600 --> 02:16:15.800
with the ketamine therapy, I realized when I came&nbsp;
out of it, I just realized that at the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:15.800 --> 02:16:23.320
day, love is the, this is going to sound cheesy,&nbsp;
but it is the most important thing that we tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:23.320 --> 02:16:28.400
each other and that we feel for each other.&nbsp;
And when you can really feel that and you say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:28.400 --> 02:16:33.920
"This person is so important to me," and&nbsp;
you let them know that and you feel it back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:33.920 --> 02:16:38.800
I don't know what's better than that.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Yeah, what's more human&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:38.800 --> 02:16:41.187
than that too, right?
Kevin Rose: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:16:41.187 --> 02:16:47.080
Tim Ferriss: In a way. I mean, love, you can&nbsp;
observe affection and love in many other species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:47.080 --> 02:16:51.840
but the ability to verbalize it and express it.
Kevin Rose: Yeah. Do you feel that psychedelics&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:51.840 --> 02:16:56.720
brings that out of you? Because when&nbsp;
I did the therapy, I had more love in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:56.720 --> 02:17:02.920
me than I've had in so long. Do you feel&nbsp;
that's a commonality amongst psychedelics&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:02.920 --> 02:17:10.880
to have a sense of just gratitude for existence?
Tim Ferriss: For sure. I think it's very common.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:11.520 --> 02:17:16.081
It's not always across the board. I mean, I would&nbsp;
say that this might be a controversial statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:16.080 --> 02:17:22.479
but the psychedelics, to me, are similar to —&nbsp;
there's a lot more to it, which we'll probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:22.479 --> 02:17:28.719
dive into, but kind of like alcohol, or power,&nbsp;
or money in the sense that they magnify what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:28.720 --> 02:17:38.640
already there and a term you sometimes hear&nbsp;
is a non-specific amplifier. So I don't think&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:38.640 --> 02:17:45.281
that at all. There's no compelling evidence to me&nbsp;
that psychedelics, if put in the drinking water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:45.280 --> 02:17:50.547
produce world peace, there's no evidence for that.&nbsp;
Right? I mean, you have plenty of civilizations —&nbsp;

02:17:50.548 --> 02:17:53.503
Kevin Rose: Maybe lithium though. No,&nbsp;
no, no, remember there was a study.&nbsp;

02:17:53.503 --> 02:17:56.308
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I remember. I remember.&nbsp;
Yeah, a little bit of lithium goes a long way.&nbsp;

02:17:56.307 --> 02:17:58.359
Kevin Rose: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, people can look&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:58.359 --> 02:18:02.160
this up. We'll try to find, there's some —
Kevin Rose: There's a study that people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:02.160 --> 02:18:06.680
populations where there is more lithium naturally&nbsp;
found in the drinking water have lower rates of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:06.680 --> 02:18:09.800
suicide than anywhere else in the world.
Tim Ferriss: All sorts of stuff. Yeah, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:09.800 --> 02:18:15.200
we'll see what we can find. Put in the show notes.&nbsp;
I do think low-dose lithium is pretty interesting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:15.200 --> 02:18:20.040
Very low dose. But psychedelics are a non-specific&nbsp;
amplifier. There are many cases of civilizations&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:20.040 --> 02:18:25.148
where they had human sacrifice, played soccer&nbsp;
with human heads, and they consumed psychedelics.&nbsp;

02:18:25.148 --> 02:18:26.920
Kevin Rose: I still sacrifice people.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah.&nbsp;

02:18:26.920 --> 02:18:29.120
Kevin Rose: You know. As we all do.
Tim Ferriss: Every once in a while in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:29.120 --> 02:18:33.680
the solstice, I mean, I think it's all fair&nbsp;
game. The feeling of gratitude and love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:33.680 --> 02:18:39.120
I think, for a lot of people who wish&nbsp;
to enable that, to experience it more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:39.120 --> 02:18:45.720
who have the conscious, or subconscious, desire&nbsp;
to rekindle those things, that the experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:45.720 --> 02:18:48.160
you're describing is very common.
Kevin Rose: Yeah. For sure.&nbsp;

02:18:48.160 --> 02:18:53.319
Tim Ferriss: I think part of that is the&nbsp;
dissolution, this isn't true of all experiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:53.319 --> 02:19:02.799
but the dissolution of self and, consequently, the&nbsp;
felt sense of unity with many or all things. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:02.800 --> 02:19:08.227
leads you to feel, for most people, less alone.
Kevin Rose: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:19:08.227 --> 02:19:13.399
Tim Ferriss: Which leads you to feel quite&nbsp;
grateful because, to state the obvious, I suppose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:14.359 --> 02:19:18.599
despite the fact that we're more connected than&nbsp;
ever with loose ties, I think a lot of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:18.600 --> 02:19:21.023
suffer from anxiety and depression —
Kevin Rose: Oh, for sure.&nbsp;

02:19:21.023 --> 02:19:25.880
Tim Ferriss: — that is highly correlated to&nbsp;
feeling of isolation. So, when you can remedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:25.880 --> 02:19:31.359
that by feeling the exact polar opposite&nbsp;
in some of these states, not necessary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:31.359 --> 02:19:38.307
but helpful, that the end result of&nbsp;
that is a feeling of gratitude for sure.&nbsp;

02:19:38.307 --> 02:19:39.559
Kevin Rose: Awesome.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:19:39.560 --> 02:19:43.360
Kevin Rose: What was the — before we wrap things&nbsp;
up, because I know we're coming to the end and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:43.359 --> 02:19:49.867
you still have some tequila to finish. What was&nbsp;
the cliffhanger you said you wanted to tell us?&nbsp;

02:19:49.867 --> 02:19:53.400
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, so I'll try to keep it short.&nbsp;
I'll let you — I'll tell you what, I'll keep it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:53.399 --> 02:19:55.223
short and then you can excavate —
Kevin Rose: Okay.&nbsp;

02:19:55.223 --> 02:19:58.867
Tim Ferriss: — as you like.
Kevin Rose: I love to excavate.&nbsp;

02:19:58.867 --> 02:20:03.000
Tim Ferriss: All right. So as you know, for&nbsp;
the last year, I've basically been crippled&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:03.000 --> 02:20:04.346
by lower back issues.
Kevin Rose: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:20:04.346 --> 02:20:07.160
Tim Ferriss: I mean, I've had trouble — there&nbsp;
are days when I've had trouble getting up&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:07.160 --> 02:20:10.760
and walking. I mean, it's been that bad.
Kevin Rose: Yeah. I mean, to be honest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:10.760 --> 02:20:16.520
Tim, you first told me about this eight years&nbsp;
ago or something. Remember when you got those&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:16.520 --> 02:20:18.041
injections or something?
Tim Ferriss: Sure.&nbsp;

02:20:18.040 --> 02:20:20.386
Kevin Rose: You were like, "I&nbsp;
think I have degenerative disc…"&nbsp;

02:20:20.386 --> 02:20:21.626
Tim Ferriss: "...disc disease."
Kevin Rose: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:20:21.627 --> 02:20:26.320
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. So I've had lower back issues&nbsp;
for a long time. This is also a congenital issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:26.319 --> 02:20:32.559
So I have what's called a transitional segment.&nbsp;
In other words, there's a segment in my lumbar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:32.560 --> 02:20:43.840
there are vertebrae in my lumbar area that&nbsp;
really mimic a sacral vertebral segment and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:43.840 --> 02:20:48.840
that's problematic for a host of reasons.&nbsp;
Creates an abnormal angle. And if you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:48.840 --> 02:20:53.600
imagine just bending the paper clip over and&nbsp;
over again in ways that it shouldn't be bent.&nbsp;

02:20:53.600 --> 02:20:58.281
That's sort of the feeling in the low back.&nbsp;
My brother has this. There are other people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:58.280 --> 02:21:04.200
in my family who have this issue. So standing&nbsp;
extended, slow walking, like a museum walk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:04.200 --> 02:21:10.040
cocktail party type of experience, can be very&nbsp;
painful, like the low back locks up. But in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:10.040 --> 02:21:20.479
last year, specifically, had all sorts of issues&nbsp;
and all sorts of MRIs and specialists, and PT, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:20.479 --> 02:21:26.479
adjustments, and traction and this, that, and the&nbsp;
other thing. And it was a disaster and it caused&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:26.479 --> 02:21:32.439
a — well, let me give myself a little more agency.&nbsp;
I created a lot of anxiety around this 'cause I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:32.439 --> 02:21:34.879
was like, "Fuck, is this the new normal?"
Kevin Rose: Right.&nbsp;

02:21:34.880 --> 02:21:39.160
Tim Ferriss: Is this really the new normal where —
Kevin Rose: Well, because you project forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:39.160 --> 02:21:41.080
what does five years from now look like?
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:41.080 --> 02:21:42.720
I'm not that old.
Kevin Rose: Right.&nbsp;

02:21:42.720 --> 02:21:45.587
Tim Ferriss: And I'm pretty active.
Kevin Rose: Yeah, and your skincare is amazing.&nbsp;

02:21:45.587 --> 02:21:47.241
Tim Ferriss: My skincare is amazing.
Kevin Rose: I saw your Instagram —&nbsp;

02:21:47.240 --> 02:21:48.706
Tim Ferriss: Oh, boy.
Kevin Rose: — video.&nbsp;

02:21:48.707 --> 02:21:55.120
Tim Ferriss: You know what's funny, sidebar,&nbsp;
is how much time you can spend on a blog post.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:55.120 --> 02:21:59.840
Takes six months to put together and it's like,&nbsp;
you get crickets and a fart in the wind and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:59.840 --> 02:22:04.240
nobody ever reads it again. And then someone&nbsp;
on your team is like, "You know what, people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:04.240 --> 02:22:08.280
ask about your skincare. Let's grab a couple&nbsp;
clips and throw it up," and it goes bananas.&nbsp;

02:22:08.280 --> 02:22:12.840
Kevin Rose: I'm on Instagram and the first thing&nbsp;
that pops up to me is like, "Hi, I'm Tim Ferriss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:12.840 --> 02:22:14.908
People ask me about my skincare regimen."
Tim Ferriss: Oh, God.&nbsp;

02:22:14.908 --> 02:22:20.040
Kevin Rose: "Let me tell you, I use Bronner's&nbsp;
Natural Soap," or whatever. It's like, "What&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:20.040 --> 02:22:22.840
the fuck has Tim become into?"
Tim Ferriss: Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:22.840 --> 02:22:26.394
so here we are, folks. Here we are, folks.
Kevin Rose: Your skin looks amazing though.&nbsp;

02:22:26.393 --> 02:22:26.738
Tim Ferriss: Thank you. Thank you.
Kevin Rose: Breathtaking.&nbsp;

02:22:26.739 --> 02:22:26.785
Tim Ferriss: It's —
Kevin Rose: All natural.&nbsp;

02:22:26.785 --> 02:22:29.360
Tim Ferriss: — it's working from the inside out.&nbsp;
So, coming back to the main throughline here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:29.359 --> 02:22:40.040
terrifying experience, the back. And I had more&nbsp;
or less given up. I was in the stage of grief&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:40.040 --> 02:22:45.120
where I was trying to get past denial and accept&nbsp;
that this might be the new normal because no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:45.120 --> 02:22:50.680
could figure it out. And a lot of the advice I&nbsp;
received, many of the diagnoses were conflicting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:50.680 --> 02:22:57.360
and then I decided, in part because of this,&nbsp;
to do a few things. And part of that was going&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:57.359 --> 02:23:01.719
to South America, which I'm not recommending,&nbsp;
there are a lot of risks down in South America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:01.720 --> 02:23:05.800
It's generally like safety fifth, it's not&nbsp;
safety first, so people getting into a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:05.800 --> 02:23:10.960
trouble. But I went down and did this training,&nbsp;
which involved consuming a bunch of plants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:10.960 --> 02:23:16.467
also involved fasting for a week, also involved —
Kevin Rose: Wait, wait, wait, wait, water fast?&nbsp;

02:23:16.466 --> 02:23:18.666
Tim Ferriss: Water fast.
Kevin Rose: Jesus. Seven days?&nbsp;

02:23:18.667 --> 02:23:25.320
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Also involved during that week,&nbsp;
really not sitting, and standing very little. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:25.319 --> 02:23:30.040
it was either hammock or cot, basically. And&nbsp;
I'm mentioning these things because they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:30.040 --> 02:23:35.760
confounders. Translating that into basic English,&nbsp;
that means that I can't really point back to one&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:35.760 --> 02:23:44.880
thing and say, "This is what caused what I'm going&nbsp;
to describe." But during the first experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:44.880 --> 02:23:50.560
which happened to be with ayahuasca, which is a&nbsp;
huge gun, it's, I think, treated very casually&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:50.560 --> 02:23:55.800
by people who do not realize what they're signing&nbsp;
up for. I do not recommend it to most people. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:55.800 --> 02:24:00.640
my ex, who's going to become relevant in the&nbsp;
second, would tell you, I talk many, many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:00.640 --> 02:24:03.823
many more people. Nine out of 10 —
Kevin Rose: Which ex?&nbsp;

02:24:03.823 --> 02:24:07.480
Tim Ferriss: — out of — I'm not going to&nbsp;
mention her name, but out my most recent ex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

